  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 320 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nCirculation from the upper-level trough can be seen via water vapor \nimagery around the AZ-Mexico border with moisture streaming out \nahead of it into the Southern and Central Plains. The ridge remains \nparked in the Gulf up through the Lower MS River Valley. Meanwhile \nanother upper trough is amplifying off the Pacific Northwest coast. \nAt the surface, a less-defined area of low pressure is noted in \nsouthwest KS and southeast CO. The first batch of showers with \nisolated thunder is making its way through north central KS where \nthe deeper moisture is located, and should continue moving north-\nnortheast through the rest of the afternoon. There looks to be a bit \nof a lull in precip early this evening before isentropic lift \nincreases and brings more scattered showers and embedded storms west \nto east later tonight. Periods of rain will continue at times Friday \nand Saturday as DCVA increases with shortwaves rounding the ridge \nand helping to increase synoptic lift. That said, coverage remains \nscattered and there may also be lulls in activity with more isolated \nrain Friday afternoon and early evening. So it will not be raining \ncontinuously at any particular location and a washout is not \nanticipated over the next couple of days. Pwat values look to be at \ntheir peak tonight into Friday (1.5-1.9\") and then decrease into \nSaturday with only a few hundred J/kg of MUCAPE to support \nconvection. That could result in some heavy downpours at times, but \nam not anticipating enough to result in major flooding concerns. In \ngeneral, hi-res guidance suggests some isolated locations could see \nrain totals of 2+ inches, though 0.5-1.5\" seems more realistic for \nmost places. \n\nThere is still some uncertainty on how soon the last shortwave \ndeparts and when exactly rain will end altogether Saturday night, \nbut overall it looks like we should be drying out by Sunday morning. \nLow-amplitude ridging favors dry weather Sunday and most of Monday, \nalthough there isn't much of a warm-up in store as low-level winds \nturn northerly and easterly. The strengthening Pacific trough then \nmakes it way across the CONUS and brings the next cold front Monday \nnight into Tuesday, which brings the next best chance for rain. \nModels diverge on the speed and amplitude of this system by \nWednesday, and PoPs continue but with lower confidence. One possible \nscenario is for a more amplified and progressive trough, while \nanother scenario is for the upper low to become cut off and meander \naround the central US through the end of the week. Either way, at \nleast somewhat cooler temperatures are forecast behind the front by \nWednesday.",
